<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Mountainous Armenia</h1><p class="st-meta">1921 CE – 1921 CE · capital Goris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An anti-Bolshevik Armenian state in Zangezur, led by Garegin Nzhdeh in 1921, whose resistance helped keep the region within Armenia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR</a> replaced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a> in December 1920, the southern mountain district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangezur">Zangezur</a>, defended by the commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garegin-nzhdeh">Garegin Nzhdeh</a>, refused to accept Soviet rule. Following the failed February 1921 uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, the anti-Bolshevik forces retreated there and in April 1921 proclaimed an autonomous <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-syunik">Syunik</a>, renamed the Republic of Mountainous <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> in June, with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goris">Goris</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was a wartime state led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garegin-nzhdeh">Nzhdeh</a> as commander and later prime minister, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-vratsian">Simon Vratsian</a> and other former leaders of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-first-republic">First Republic</a>. It relied on local militias and the mountainous terrain of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-syunik">Syunik</a> and controlled a population swollen with refugees. It sought union with the rest of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> once free of Bolshevik rule.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In July 1921, after the Soviets reportedly promised to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangezur">Zangezur</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenian-ssr">Soviet Armenia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took the region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garegin-nzhdeh">Nzhdeh</a> withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The area remained part of the Armenian SSR.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-mountainous-armenia.jpg" alt="Republic of Mountainous Armenia" width="320" height="219"><figcaption>Image: Hesov · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountainous_Armenia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1921 CE – 1921 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Goris</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Garegin Nzhdeh, Simon Vratsian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Apostolic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Armenia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mountainous Armenia, Lernahayastan</dd></dl></aside></div>
